uses X already set. Let me find \"STX\" or \"LDX\" earlier. Let me just disassemble.\n\nLet me also consider: the table might be indexed directly by enemy type*6? Goomba type 6 → 6*6=36 → 0x24 offset → CPU $E762. Hmm, not 0x54.\n\nGoomba offset 0x54 = 84 = 14*6. So if index = type*6, type=14. Hmm, so Goomba type would be 14 (0x0E)? But mapping says Goomba enemy_type = 6. Hmm. So not type*6.\n\nWait, mapping says $0016 enemy TYPE/ID array; World 1-1 Goomba holds 6. And writing 11 made a red Koopa. So type 6 = Goomba, type 11 = red Koopa.\n\nGoomba row at offset 0x54. Red Koopa row should be A5-A9 = offset 0x674A-0x673E = 0x0C. Hmm. So type 6 → 0x54, type 11 → 0x0C? That would need a lookup table.\n\nLet me find that lookup table. It's probably \"EnemyGfxTable\" or \"EnemyGfxOffsets\". Let me search for a table that has 0x54 at index 6 and 0x0C at index 11. Hmm, hard to search.\n\nBetter: disassemble the routine to see how X is set.\n\nLet me disassemble from $E940.\n\nLet me do it."}
