{"type":"thinking","text":"Now I see the structure. At $E8DE: LDA $E878,X → $EF (enemy type from table $E878 indexed by $070E, and Y... hmm). Then $EF is the enemy type used throughout.\n\nAt $E92A: LDA $E840,Y → X. So $E840 is a table of per-enemy-type graphic base offsets (byte offsets into the tile-pair table at $E73E)! Y = $EF (enemy type). So X = $E840[type].\n\nThen various conditions add 6 (at $EA32: ADC #$06) or set specific X values.\n\nSo $E840 is the \"EnemyGfxTable offsets\" table: $E840[type] = offset into the row table at $E73E.\n\nSo for Goomba (type 6): X = $E840[6]. The Goomba row is at offset 0x54 from $E73E. So $E840[6] should be 0x54. Let me verify: we saw at $E9BE: LDX #$54 when CMP $06 → yes! For type 6 (Goomba), X=$54. \n\nAt $E9BE: `LDX #$54` when `cpy #$06` (type==6). And offset 0x54 → CPU $E792 → prgRom 0x6792 = Goomba row ✓✓. \n\nSo the routine special-cases type 6 → offset $54. Other types come from $E840[type].\n\nAlso:\n- $E9B6: LDX #$66 when type != 2... hmm. $E9B0-$E9B8: `LDY $EF; CPY #$02; BEQ +; LDX #$66; INC $02`. So for types other than 2 at that point, X=$66. Offset 0x66 → CPU $E7A4 → prgRom 0x67A4 = row 18 (6F 6F 6E 6E). Hmm interesting.\n- $E9AC: LDX #$72 (offset 0x72 → prgRom 0x67B0 = row 20 F4 F4 F5 F5)\n- $E9A2: LDX #$7E (offset 0x7E → prgRom 0x67BC = row 22 F5 F5 F4 F4)\n- $E99A: LDX #$5A (0x5A → prgRom 0x6798 = row 16 6E 6E 6F 6F)\n- $E98B: LDX #$96 (0x96 → prgRom 0x67D4 = row 26 FC FC BD BD BC BC)\n- $E953: LDX #$E4 (0xE4 → prgRom 0x6822 = row 39 C4 C3 C6 C5 CC CB)\n- $E93E: LDX #$DE (0xDE → prgRom 0x681C = row 38 BF BE CA C9 C2 FC)\n- $E9C6: LDX #$8A (0x8A → prgRom 0x67C8 = row 24 FC FC FC FC EF EF)\n- $E9DA: LDX #$B4 (0xB4 → prgRom 0x67F2 = row 31 D5 D4 E3 E2 D3 D2)\n- $E96D: LDX #$30 (0x30 → prgRom 0x676E = row 9 FC FC 8F 8E 8E 8F)\n\nAnd $E840[type] table gives base offsets. Let me read $E840 table (prgRom 0x6840). Recall the bytes at 0x6840 onward: 12,12,0,12,12,168,84,60,234,24,72,72,204,192,24,24,24,144,36,255,72,156,210,216,240,246,252,1,2,3,2,1,1,3,3,3,1,1,2,2,33,1,2,1,1,2,255,2,2,1,1,2,2,2,8,24,24,25,26,25,24,181,207,...\n\nHmm, $E840 = prgRom 0x6840. The first bytes: 12,12,0,12,12,168,84,60,234,24,72,72,204,192,24,24,24,144,36,255,72,156,210,216,240,246,252,1,2,3,2,1,1,3,3,3,1,1,2,2,33,1,2,1,1,2,255,...\n\nHmm these are byte offsets: e.g., index 0 = 12 (0x0C), index 1 = 12, index 2 = 0, index 3 = 12, index 4 = 12, index 5 = 168 (0xA8), index 6 = 84 (0x54) ✓ Goomba!, index 7 = 60 (0x3C), index 8 = 234 (0xEA), index 9 = 24 (0x18), index 10 = 72 (0x48), index 11 = 72 (0x48), index 12 = 204 (0xCC), index 13 = 192 (0xC0), index 14 = 24, index 15 = 24, index 16 = 24, index 17 = 144 (0x90), index 18 = 36 (0x24), index 19 = 255 (0xFF!), index 20 = 72 (0x48), index 21 = 156 (0x9C), index 22 = 210 (0xD2), index 23 = 216 (0xD8), index 24 = 240 (0xF0), index 25 = 246 (0xF6), index 26 = 252 (0xFC)...\n\nHmm wait, but is $E840 the table? At $E92A: `LDA $E840,Y / TAX` with Y = $EF (enemy type). Yes.\n\nBut careful: the base table might be at $E840 and indexed by Y = $EF, where $EF is the enemy type. Let me list:\ntype 0: 0x0C\ntype 1: 0x0C\ntype 2: 0x00\ntype 3: 0x0C\ntype 4: 0x0C\ntype 5: 0xA8\ntype 6: 0x54  (Goomba ✓)\ntype 7: 0x3C\ntype 8: 0xEA\ntype 9: 0x18\ntype 10: 0x48\ntype 11: 0x48\ntype 12: 0xCC\ntype 13: 0xC0\ntype 14: 0x18\ntype 15: 0x18\ntype 16: 0x18\ntype 17: 0x90\ntype 18: 0x24\ntype 19: 0xFF\ntype 20: 0x48\ntype 21: 0x9C\ntype 22: 0xD2\ntype 23: 0xD8\ntype 24: 0xF0\ntype 25: 0xF6\ntype 26: 0xFC\n...\n\nHmm but wait: $EF is the enemy type? Earlier at $E8FE: `STY $EF` with Y=$16 or $17 based on $036A. And at $E8E1: `STA $EF` from $E878,X. Hmm, so $EF = enemy type (the \"enemy gfx type\"?). Actually $EF might be the *enemy's sprite/form index*, not the raw type. Hmm.\n\nHmm, $E878 is a table indexed by $070E. Let me look. Actually at $E8D9: LDY #$03; LDX $070E; LDA $E878,X; STA $EF; STY $EC. So $EF = $E878[$070E]. Hmm, $070E might be the enemy's \"type\" or a sub-index.\n\nThis is getting deep. But the key table $E840[type] → offset gives us row→type mapping, IF $EF is the enemy type. And the special cases in the routine likewise.\n\nNow, more useful: the routine's special-case offsets tell us the rows for specific enemy types:\n- type 6 → 0x54 (Goomba): row 15 (0x6792) ✓\n- type 2 → offset from table = 0x00 → row 1 (0x673E)\n- type 9 → 0x18 → prgRom 0x6756 = row 5 (69 A5 6A A7 A8 A9)\n- type 18 → 0x24 → prgRom 0x6762 = row 7 (96 97 98 99)\n- type 7 → 0x3C → prgRom 0x677A = row 11 (FC FC DC DC DF DF)\n- type 10/11/20 → 0x48 → prgRom 0x6786 = row 13 (FC FC B2 B3 B4 B5)\n- type 5 → 0xA8 → prgRom 0x67E6 = row 29 (7D 7C D1 8C D3 D2)\n- type 8 → 0xEA → prgRom 0x6828 = row 40 (FC FC E8 E7 EA E9)\n- type 12 → 0xCC → prgRom 0x680A = row 35 (FC FC D0 D0 D7 D7)\n- type 13 → 0xC0 → prgRom 0x67FE = row 33 (E5 E5 E6 E6 EB EB)\n- type 14/15/16 → 0x18 → row 5 (69 A5 6A A7 A8 A9)\n- type 17 → 0x90 → prgRom 0x67CE = row 25 (B9 B8 BB BA BC BC)\n- type 21 → 0x9C → prgRom 0x67DA = row 27 (7A 7B DA DB D8 D8)\n- type 22 → 0xD2 → prgRom 0x6810 = row 36 (BF BE C1 C0 C2 FC)\n- type 23 → 0xD8 → prgRom 0x6816 = row 37 (C4 C3 C6 C5 C8 C7)\n- type 24 → 0xF0 → prgRom 0x682E = row 41 (F2 F2 F3 F3 F2 F2)\n- type 25 → 0xF6 → prgRom 0x6834 = row 42 (F1 F1 F1 F1 FC FC)\n- type 26 → 0xFC → prgRom 0x683A = row 43 (F0 F0 FC FC FC FC)\n\nHmm, but these offsets must be reproducible. But wait, is $E840 really the base table, or is it a different table? Let me verify by reading memory at $E840 and confirm type 6 = 0x54. From my earlier read starting 0x66E0, index i for 0x6840 = 0x6840-0x66E0 = 0x160 = 352. Bytes i=352... = 12,12,0,12,12,168,... So $E840[0]=12, [1]=12, [2]=0, [3]=12, [4]=12, [5]=168, [6]=84 ✓, [7]=60, [8]=234, [9]=24, [10]=72, [11]=72, [12]=204, [13]=192, [14]=24, [15]=24, [16]=24, [17]=144, [18]=36, [19]=255, [20]=72, [21]=156, [22]=210, [23]=216, [24]=240, [25]=246, [26]=252.\n\nHmm wait, but the bytes at 0x6840 might be at i=352 (0x6840-0x66E0=352). Let me